! Model lumping through ElasticSolve, by six pure forces and moments -- the
! "Fix Displacement = False" method. The sibling beam-springs is the same beam,
! the same lumping boundary and the same reference norm through StressSolve.
!
! WHY THIS DIRECTORY EXISTS. Model lumping was extracted out of StressSolve into
! its own module, ModelLumping.F90, as SHARED code -- so that both solvers could
! call it -- and then only StressSolve ever did. Four call sites there, none here,
! and "Model Lumping = Logical True" was silently inert in ElasticSolve: the six
! load cases never happened, nothing loaded the body, and the displacement norm
! came back as exactly 0.0 against a reference of 2.9e-2. Wiring an already shared
! module into its second caller, which is all this was, is the cheapest item the
! retirement survey found.
!
! WHAT THE TEST ACTUALLY PROVES is stronger than its norm. The product of the
! feature is the lumped 6x6 spring matrix in kmat.dat, and the two solvers agree on
! it to ALL NINE printed digits of every physically meaningful entry -- 1.03978080,
! 100.399049, 34.9290787, 5.60042068, and the +-5.23230714 / +-5.21760327 couplings.
! What differs is confined to the entries that are numerically zero, at 1e-12
! against a diagonal of order 1 to 100, which is the round-off of the linear solve.
!
! THIS BRANCH exercises ModelLumpingLoads, which builds each case as a pure force
! or a pure moment over the boundary and OVERWRITES whatever surface load was read
! there. The sibling ElasticModelLumpingFixdisp covers the other branch, where each
! case is a prescribed displacement instead and a different routine imposes it.
!
! Two things the driver has to get right, neither of them visible here:
! the six cases ride the nonlinear iteration counter, so both bounds are forced to
! six and no convergence test may stop early; and the reactions are recovered from
! BulkValues times the solution, so "Constant Bulk System" is added to the keyword
! list rather than assumed -- it is what makes DefaultFinishBulkAssembly save them.
!
! Small deflection, as the lumped stiffness is a linear concept and the six cases
! are load cases rather than Newton steps.

Header
  CHECK KEYWORDS Warn
  Mesh DB "." "beam3d"
  Include Path ""
  Results Directory ""
End

Simulation
! Max Output Level = 5

  Coordinate System = "Cartesian 3D"
  Coordinate Mapping(3) = 1 2 3

  Simulation Type = "Steady State"
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
  Steady State Min Iterations = 1
  Output Intervals = 1

! Output File = "beam3d.dat"
! Post File = "beam3d.ep"
End

Constants
  Gravity(4) = 0 -1 0 9.82
  Stefan Boltzmann = 5.67e-08
End

Body 1
  Name = "Body1"

  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
End

Equation 1
  Name = "Equation1"

  Stress Analysis = True
  Calculate Stresses = Logical False
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "Stress Analysis"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Large Deflection = False
  Variable = -dofs 3 "Displacement"

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = umfpack

  Constant Bulk System = Logical True
  Linear System Refactorize = Logical False

  Model Lumping = Logical True
  Model Lumping Filename = String "kmat.dat"

  Nonlinear System Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-05
  Nonlinear System Relaxation Factor = 1.0
  Nonlinear System Min Iterations = Integer 6
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = Integer 6
  Time Derivative Order = 2

  Fix Displacement = Logical False
End


Material 1
  Name = "Material1"

  Youngs Modulus = 1.0e3
  Poisson Ratio = 0.3
  Density = 0.0
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Name = "wall_end"
  Target Boundaries(1) = 5

! The beam is fixed at the one end
  Displacement 1 = 0
  Displacement 2 = 0
  Displacement 3 = 0
End

Boundary Condition 2
  Name = "free_end"
  Target Boundaries(1) = 6

! The force is permutated in three steps through the main directions.
  Model Lumping Boundary = Logical True
End



Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 0.28897176E-01
RUN
